# Connection loss and recovery

Distinguish the browser’s connection to Home Assistant from an integration’s connection and an entity’s missing state. A single unavailable entity must not turn the whole application into an offline screen. Retained data remain useful only when their age and uncertainty are visible.

## Scope and native ownership

The native WebSocket connection authenticates and carries commands/results and event subscriptions. Entity unknown/unavailable meanings come from the separate state model. The companion must preserve native connection handling, authentication and diagnostics. [Official Home Assistant source](https://contributors.home-assistant.io/core/internals/websocket-api/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-018**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Browser connection lost | Name Home Assistant connection lost and retain labelled last reports. | Do not call every device offline. |
| Reconnecting | Show a persistent status without repeated interruptions. | Do not replay consequential requests. |
| Connection restored | Refresh from native subscriptions and reconcile pending results. | A socket opening alone does not confirm an earlier operation. |
| One entity unavailable | Explain that the entity cannot supply its state. | Do not diagnose Wi-Fi failure without evidence. |
| Authentication rejected | Use the native sign-in/re-authentication route. | Do not collect tokens inside a themed error card. |

## Integration contract

1. Use the native connection and subscription lifecycle. Do not open a second device-control connection to support appearance changes.
2. Track the pending request separately from last reported state. A lost response may leave an action completed, pending or rejected; no timeout can prove which occurred.
3. Keep non-secret draft inputs only where the editor actually supports retention. Reauthentication and navigation can have different persistence limits; describe them accurately.
4. In safe/recovery mode, native controls and branding take precedence. Missing adapter targets must fail without removing connection messages or the native theme selector.

## Accessible interaction

- Use a polite persistent status for routine reconnecting and announce meaningful transitions once. Do not place the entire updating dashboard in a live region.
- Keep focus on the interrupted task unless authentication requires a new context; preserve a useful return path when possible.
- Provide recovery text beside disabled actions. Colour and a spinning icon alone cannot explain which task is unavailable.

## Failure and recovery

- A retry link must perform a real supported retry. Otherwise link to instructions and label it accordingly.
- After reconnect, read the current reported state before asking the user to repeat a potentially completed device action.
- Keep failures from native services visible. Never suppress diagnostics merely because a theme adapter stopped attaching.

